\begin_layout Subsection
Outputting Mega2R data to VCF format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
Mega2VCF
\emph default
 function can output subsets of the Mega2R database as a VCF file, accompanied
 by related files (for phenotype and family data).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Writing a VCF File using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, create the directory "vcfr", so we can keep all the generated data
 in one place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<mega2vcflib>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

if (! dir.exists("vcfr")) dir.create("vcfr")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
Mega2VCF
\emph default
 function takes an argument which is the prefix used on all generated files.
 We will make it a path that includes the directory 
\series bold
vcfr
\series default
.
 Below, we assume that a Mega2 database was stored in the environment, 'ENV'.
 If this is not the case, supply to 
\emph on
Mega2VCF
\emph default
 a named argument, 'envir', set to the environment you wish to use.
 (You will also have to change the references to the 
\series bold
markers
\series default
 data frame, below, from 'ENV'$markers to '<environment name>'$markers.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that Mega2VCF will create the .vcf file as well as a .fam file (in linkage
 format), a .phe file (phenotypes) and a few others.
 These files are listed below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Creating the VCF file and related files is accomplished by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<mega2vcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2VCF("vcfr/vcf.01", ENV$markers[ENV$markers$chromosome==1 ,])
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The line above places the data for only chromosome 1 in the files
 (Recall that our simulated data only contains chromosome 1).
 
\color black
It is worth noting that the internal logic does not create a genotype matrix
 for all the requested markers at once, but rather works on a block of 1000
 markers at a time, limiting the amount of memory required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: In general, you can filter 'ENV$markers' as needed.
 If the second argument is not present, all the markers are written out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The generated files are listed below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<lsvcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

list.files("vcfr")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Converting Mega2R data to GenABEL format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides support for converting the data frames into 'GenABEL' (https://c
ran.r-project.org/package=GenABEL) format (e.g.
 as gwaa.data-class objects).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Creating a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several data formats that Mega2 can read and transform into a
 database that are not directly accepted by GenABEL.
 But any Mega2 database 
\change_deleted -1820302391 1508530281
that
\change_unchanged
 can be read into R, 
\change_inserted -1820302391 1508530288
and 
\change_unchanged
can be transformed to GenABEL following this example using 
\series bold
seqsimr.db
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GenABEL (http://www.genabel.org/) can process PLINK .tped/.tfam files with the
 function 'convert.snp.tped' to create a GenABEL "raw" file.
 Then the "raw" file and a generated phenotype file can be processed by
 the function, 'load.gwaa.data', to yield a gwaa.data-class object.
 Currently, the function, 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
, uses this mechanism to convert Mega2 data frames to a gwaa.data-class object.
 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 generates the names of the .tped/.fam/.phe files in 
\change_deleted -1820302391 1508530374
the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tempdir()
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\change_inserted -1820302391 1508530382
a temporary
\change_unchanged
 directory with a 
\change_inserted -1820302391 1508530550
file name 
\change_unchanged
prefix of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Mega2GenABEL
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The GenABEL functions 'convert.snp.tped' and 'load.gwaa.data' are explained
 in https://cran.r-project.org/package=GenABEL.
 When 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL 
\emph default
is finished, it deletes all the files that it generated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you have not done so already, install the 'GenABEL' package from CRAN.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# This line converts the database to a gwaa.data-class object.
 The intermediate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# files in the tempdir() directory begin with "Mega2GenABEL"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

seqsimgwaa = Mega2GenABEL()
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can use any of the GenABEL provided functions on the results, 'seqsimgwaa'.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<strseqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

str(seqsimgwaa)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

