\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These results are somewhat disappointing.
 But ...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, "mega2" prefers to use the pedigree id concatenated to the person
 id as the sample name, even if the person id itself is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second difference, in 
\series bold
gtdata@strand,
\series default
 occurs because Mega2 does not keep track of strand orientation and always
 returns 0.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final difference is more subtle.
 The
\series bold
 gtdata
\series default
 comparison line decodes the genotype to characters then normalizes the
 two heterozygous cases to one value.
 These results are the same for the two different gwaa.class-objects; hence
 the data are really the same.
 But 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 converts the data frames using the 'convert.snp.tped' function while the
 'srdta' object, generated by GenABEL, uses the 'convert.snp.text'.
 Though both conversion functions encode the genotype to a number, the 'convert.s
np.tped' function rearranges the genotype so that the major allele appears
 first, the 'convert.snp.text' does no such rearrangement.
 Because 
\series bold
gtdata@coding
\series default
 and 
\series bold
gtdata@gtps
\series default
 compare the encoded allele pairs and corresponding encoded genotypes, they
 sometimes differ.
\end_layout

